Ladybird exposes a cross-origin child on its parent’s window by name
only when the child’s target name matches its container’s name
attribute, which it replicates to every process representing the tab.
Blink does the same: WindowProperties::AnonymousNamedGetter() exposes
a cross-origin child only when the name equals its owner’s
BrowsingContextContainerName(), which other processes get from
FrameOwnerProperties::name, in Frame::ApplyFrameOwnerProperties().
Gecko and WebKit don’t check the container’s name at all: Gecko’s
RemoteOuterWindowProxy::getOwnPropertyDescriptor() and WebKit’s
jsDOMWindowGetOwnPropertySlotRestrictedAccess() expose any child whose
own name matches. Ladybird follows Blink and the spec, so a name that
a cross-origin child gives itself through window.name stays hidden.
